window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["cj7"]=[{"id":"front-bumper-cj7","title":"Steel Front Bumper \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":240,"high":720},"tools_required":["floor jack","socket set","torque wrench","drill with bits","wire brush","rust penetrant"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["armor","bumper","front","winch","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt SRC Gen2 front bumper (CJ-7)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":310},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 front bumper with winch plate (CJ-7)","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Rugged Ridge tube bumper with winch mount (CJ-7)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Frame rail gusset plates (pair)","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Front Bumper Buyers Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj7-bumpers.htm"}],"alternatives":["rear-bumper-cj7","rock-sliders-cj7","winch-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7's later production run and greater overall numbers mean the aftermarket bumper selection is significantly wider than for the CJ-5 \u2014 but the frame rail gusset requirement for winch-capable bumpers still applies.","description":"Because the CJ-7 was produced in larger numbers and more recently than the CJ-5, the aftermarket has had more reason to develop CJ-7-specific products. Smittybilt's SRC Gen2, Rock Hard 4x4's tube bumper with winch plate, and Rugged Ridge's tube bumper are all purpose-built CJ-7 fits with documented mounting hardware. This is worth noting because CJ-5 and CJ-7 bumpers are not interchangeable \u2014 the frame rail width, front body width, and mounting hole geometry differ between the two models.\n\nA winch-capable bumper is the right choice for a CJ-7 trail build. The CJ-7 is the first CJ generation where a winch is a practical primary recovery tool rather than a secondary option \u2014 the 93.5-inch wheelbase and better weight distribution make it more appropriate than on the shorter CJ-5.\n\nThe frame rail capacity issue is identical to the CJ-5: the front frame rails are thin and not designed to carry a loaded winch. For a winch-capable bumper over 80 lbs, weld or bolt gusset plates across both frame rails before mounting. RuffStuff Specialties bolt-on gussets ($85/pair) work without a welder. This step is not optional on a bumper that will carry a winch under full recovery load.\n\nRock Hard 4x4's bumper for the CJ-7 ($580) is the premium option \u2014 it uses heavier wall tube, has integrated recovery points rated for a snatch strap, and has a properly gusseted winch mount. For a CJ-7 that will see regular trail use and winch use, the Rock Hard unit is worth the premium over lower-cost options that use thinner tube and thinner mounting flanges.\n\nThe same installation procedure as the CJ-5 front bumper: remove factory bumper, clean and treat frame rail ends, install new bumper with grade-8 hardware torqued to 50\u201360 ft-lbs. Fit the winch to the bumper before mounting the bumper to the frame."},{"id":"rear-bumper-cj7","title":"Rear Bumper with Swing-Out Tire Carrier \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":380,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","socket set","torque wrench","drill with bits"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["armor","bumper","rear","tire-carrier","swing-out","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt SRC rear bumper with tire carrier (CJ-7)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":460},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 rear bumper with swing-out (CJ-7)","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"Rugged Ridge rear bumper with tire mount (CJ-7)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":410}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Rear Bumper and Tire Carrier Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj7-rear-bumper.htm"}],"alternatives":["front-bumper-cj7","rock-sliders-cj7"],"summary":"Unlike the CJ-5, the CJ-7's longer body gives the rear frame enough overhang to support a swing-out tire carrier without major frame extension \u2014 a proper swing-out is a realistic build for the CJ-7.","description":"The CJ-7's longer wheelbase and longer rear body section put more real estate between the rear axle centerline and the rear frame ends. This gives a swing-out tire carrier enough moment arm to pivot and enough structural mounting area to anchor. Smittybilt, Rock Hard 4x4, and Rugged Ridge all make CJ-7-specific rear bumpers with integrated swing-out carriers that bolt to the factory frame holes.\n\nThis is a meaningful difference from the CJ-5, where a swing-out requires frame extension work. On the CJ-7, it is a bolt-on modification with a weekend's worth of installation time.\n\nThe swing-out carrier must be rated for the spare tire you intend to carry. A 33x12.5 tire on a 15x8 steel wheel weighs approximately 65\u201370 lbs. The carrier's hinge and latch must handle that weight repeatedly through rough terrain \u2014 cheap hinges develop slop and allow the carrier to bounce, which accelerates wear at the latch and eventually allows the door to open on trail.\n\nRock Hard 4x4's rear bumper ($680) uses heavier-duty hinge hardware and a positive latch that locks the carrier closed. The Smittybilt SRC ($460) is functional and a better value if budget is the constraint, but inspect the hinge quality before purchasing.\n\nThe carrier is farther from the tailgate on the CJ-7 than on a modern Wrangler due to body geometry \u2014 confirm the latch and hinge dimensions allow clearance when the gate opens without the tire carrier fouling the tailgate.\n\nThe rear bumper must have rated recovery loops \u2014 any CJ-7 trail bumper without rear recovery points is incomplete. Confirm the loops are fully welded to the main bumper tube structure, not tack-welded to a flange. Both loops are needed; recovery from the rear requires a centered or dual-point attachment to prevent side-loading the frame."},{"id":"rock-sliders-cj7","title":"Rock Sliders \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"armor","subcategory":"sliders","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":320,"high":820},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","angle grinder","wire brush","drill with bits"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["armor","rock-sliders","body-protection","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Poison Spyder Rock Sliders (CJ-7)","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"GenRight CJ-7 tube sliders (pair)","vendor":"GenRight Off Road","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Affordable Offroad CJ-7 sliders (pair)","vendor":"Affordable Offroad","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Rock Slider Options \u2014 Poison Spyder","url":"https://www.poisonspyder.com/cj7-rock-sliders"}],"alternatives":["skid-plates-cj7","front-bumper-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7's body tub is longer than the CJ-5 and has more sill to protect \u2014 a frame-mounted tube slider keeps rock contact away from the thin sheet metal that is the side of the CJ body.","description":"The CJ-7 body tub has a longer sill area than the CJ-5 \u2014 the extra 10 inches of wheelbase shows up in the doorway and the rear body section. That longer flat panel is more exposed to rock contact during trail driving. The body tub sides are stamped thin steel with no structural rocker panel reinforcement \u2014 they dent on contact with a rock edge at very low speed.\n\nFrame-mounted tube sliders protect both the body and the frame rail. The slider tube sits outboard of the frame, absorbing lateral rock contact and sliding over ledges rather than catching. On a well-built CJ-7, sliders are as important as skid plates \u2014 the rig's geometry and short wheelbase put the sides in contact with obstacles regularly.\n\nPoison Spyder makes CJ-7-specific rock sliders ($520) with well-documented fitment and quality welding. They use 1.75-inch DOM tube in a triangulated design that mounts to the frame through the body tub with large backing plates. GenRight's sliders ($480) are similar quality with slightly different geometry that some builders prefer for fitment with wider tire builds.\n\nAffordable Offroad offers a budget entry ($320) that is functional but uses thinner tube than the premium options \u2014 appropriate for a mild trail rig, less appropriate for serious rock work where the sliders are taking deliberate hits repeatedly.\n\nThe installation is the same as CJ-5 sliders: drill through the body tub to the frame, use large backing plates inside the tub to prevent pull-through under load, torque to 45\u201355 ft-lbs. Treat the frame surface before installation to prevent moisture trapping and rust under the sliders.\n\nThe CJ-7's longer body means the slider spans a slightly longer section of the sill \u2014 confirm the kit includes all mounting hardware for both the front door area and the rear body section."},{"id":"skid-plates-cj7","title":"Skid Plate Kit \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"armor","subcategory":"skids","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":580},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","angle grinder","torque wrench","drill with bits"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["armor","skid-plates","belly-protection","transfer-case","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 CJ-7 belly skid kit (3-piece)","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Skid Row Offroad CJ-7 full belly skid","vendor":"Skid Row Offroad","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Omix-ADA Dana 300 transfer case skid (CJ-7)","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":155},{"name":"CJ-7 oil pan skid plate (3/16\" steel)","vendor":"GenRight Off Road","price_estimate_usd":170}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Underbody Protection Guide \u2014 Rock Hard 4x4","url":"https://www.rockhard4x4.com/cj7-skid-plates"}],"alternatives":["rock-sliders-cj7","front-bumper-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7's factory undercarriage has no protection for the oil pan, transfer case, or gas tank \u2014 all three are exposed and vulnerable on trail terrain.","description":"The CJ-7 has the same undercarriage exposure problem as the CJ-5: the AMC engine's oil pan hangs low, the transfer case (Dana 20 or Dana 300) sits mid-body with low-hanging output housings, and the gas tank is rearward with minimal protection from the tank straps.\n\nThe Dana 300 transfer case, if your CJ-7 has it, is mounted slightly lower than the Dana 20 in some CJ-7 build configurations \u2014 and it's a more expensive component to repair if struck. Protecting the D300 is a priority. The oil pan on the AMC 258 is equally exposed and a rock hit that punches the pan means an immediate trail-end.\n\nRock Hard 4x4's 3-piece CJ-7 belly skid kit ($420) covers oil pan, transfer case, and gas tank in 3/16-inch steel with a modular bolt-on design \u2014 each section can be installed or removed independently. This is the most complete bolt-on solution for a CJ-7 without fabrication access. Skid Row Offroad makes a comparable kit at a slightly lower price point ($380).\n\nFor the Dana 300 specifically, Omix-ADA makes a standalone skid plate ($155) \u2014 if only the T-case is your priority, this is a good first buy. The Dana 300 is worth protecting: a used replacement is $400\u2013$800 and the unit is not trivial to swap on the trail.\n\n3/16-inch steel is the minimum appropriate material for a CJ-7 trail skid. It will deflect and slide over rock obstacles rather than folding. 1/4-inch is the better spec for a dedicated rock crawler where the skid will take deliberate impacts from ledges. Aluminum skid plates are not recommended on a CJ-7 used for serious rock work \u2014 they dent and deform permanently under repeated hard use."},{"id":"amc-20-problem-cj7","title":"The AMC 20 Rear Axle \u2014 Known Failure and the Fix \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"axles","subcategory":"rear-axle","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":380,"high":850},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","bearing press","torque wrench","dial indicator","slide hammer","snap ring pliers"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","differential service experience"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["axles","amc-20","rear-axle","two-piece-shaft","c-clip","failure","critical","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Superior Axle one-piece shaft conversion kit (AMC 20)","vendor":"Superior Axle & Gear","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Alloy USA one-piece chromoly shafts (AMC 20, pair)","vendor":"Alloy USA","price_estimate_usd":390},{"name":"Auburn ECTED open carrier (AMC 20)","vendor":"Auburn Gear","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"AMC 20 master overhaul kit","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":110}],"citations":[{"title":"AMC 20 Axle Shaft Failure Analysis \u2014 Pirate4x4","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/amc20-failure/"},{"title":"AMC 20 One-Piece Shaft Conversion \u2014 Superior Axle","url":"https://www.superioraxle.com/amc20-conversion/"},{"title":"CJ-7 Rear Axle Guide \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/amc20/"}],"alternatives":["dana-44-rear-swap-cj7","lockers-cj7","dana-30-front-cj7"],"summary":"The AMC 20 rear axle's two-piece shaft design is the single most important thing to know about the CJ-7. Under hard torque with a locker installed, the outer shaft snaps and the wheel exits the housing \u2014 taking the tire with it.","description":"Most CJ-7s left the factory with an AMC 20 rear axle \u2014 a unit with an 8.875-inch ring gear (a decent size) and a two-piece axle shaft design retained by C-clips at the differential center. The ring gear and housing are sturdy. The axle shafts are the problem.\n\nThe inner shaft and outer stub shaft are two separate pieces connected at a splined joint. The outer stub is retained by a C-clip that sits inside the differential carrier. Under normal open-differential driving, the design is adequate. Under the torque spike of a locked differential \u2014 especially with aggressive throttle, wheel hop, or a sudden traction catch \u2014 the outer stub shaft snaps at the C-clip groove. The shaft exits the housing. The wheel, still attached to the stub, goes with it.\n\nThis is not a hypothetical failure mode. It is a documented failure pattern that has been known in the CJ-7 community for decades. Every experienced CJ-7 builder knows about it. The consequence of this failure on the trail ranges from \"you're stuck\" to \"you've lost a rear wheel at speed.\" Do not install a locker on a stock AMC 20 axle.\n\nSuperior Axle & Gear and Alloy USA both make one-piece chromoly shaft conversion kits for the AMC 20. The conversion replaces the two-piece shaft assembly with a single chromoly shaft that runs from the wheel flange to the differential side gear. With a one-piece shaft, there is no C-clip retention at the differential and no failure point at the splined joint.\n\nThe conversion requires replacing the open carrier with one designed for full-length shafts \u2014 Auburn's ECTED carrier ($145) is the common choice. The complete Superior Axle kit ($420) includes shafts, carrier, and all required hardware.\n\nThe AMC 20's 8.875-inch ring gear is a real asset. It supports locker options, good ring-and-pinion availability, and reasonable strength for a CJ-7 on 33-inch tires. Once the shaft problem is solved with one-piece chromoly conversion, the AMC 20 is a usable axle for a moderate trail build. The decision to swap to a Dana 44 (see `dana-44-rear-swap-cj7`) is justified for a high-build rock crawler; for a trail rig on 33s with a locker, the corrected AMC 20 is adequate."},{"id":"dana-30-front-cj7","title":"Dana 30 Front Axle \u2014 Service and Locker Options \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"axles","subcategory":"front-axle","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":1200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","bearing press","dial indicator","ring gear holder","seal driver set"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","differential service experience"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["axles","dana-30","front-axle","locker","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right (Dana 30)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 30)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"OX Locker (Dana 30)","vendor":"OX Locker","price_estimate_usd":780},{"name":"Yukon chromoly front shaft set (Dana 30, HP30)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":210},{"name":"Dana 30 master overhaul kit","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":95}],"citations":[{"title":"Dana 30 Front Axle Guide \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/dana30/"},{"title":"CJ-7 Front Locker Options \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj7-lockers.htm"}],"alternatives":["amc-20-problem-cj7","lockers-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7's Dana 30 front axle (high-pinion, HP30) is the same unit used in the XJ Cherokee and TJ Wrangler \u2014 excellent aftermarket support, known upgrade path, and well-documented locker options.","description":"The CJ-7 came standard with a Dana 30 front axle from the start of production in 1976. Unlike the CJ-5, which used the weaker Dana 27 in early production, the CJ-7 always used the Dana 30 \u2014 and specifically the high-pinion variant (HP30) that positions the pinion above the ring gear centerline, giving better ground clearance under the housing.\n\nThis axle's specifications match the XJ Cherokee and TJ Wrangler Dana 30 in shaft dimensions and carrier design, which means every locker and upgrade part developed for those widely popular vehicles also works on the CJ-7. The parts ecosystem is excellent.\n\nSame options as the CJ-5 Dana 30: Powertrax Lock-Right ($280, lunch-box, no carrier swap), ARB Air Locker ($680, selectable, requires air), OX Locker ($780, cable-selectable, no air needed). The choice depends on how much pavement driving the CJ-7 sees and whether you want a driver-selectable system.\n\nBefore installing a front locker, upgrade to Yukon chromoly front shafts ($210). The stock Dana 30 shafts handle 33-inch tires without a locker, but a locked front axle puts higher torque through the shafts than the factory spec anticipated.\n\nAn important build sequencing point: if your CJ-7 still has the stock two-piece AMC 20 rear axle shafts, fix those first (see `amc-20-problem-cj7`) before adding a front locker. A front locker on a CJ-7 with a stock AMC 20 rear means you can apply full locked front-axle torque with a rear axle that may shed a shaft under the corresponding rear torque. The rear axle fix is the prerequisite for the full locked build.\n\nPull the differential cover and inspect bearing condition and gear mesh pattern before installing a locker. A worn differential accelerates wear on any locker \u2014 especially a lunch-box style that relies on the existing carrier bearing surfaces. If the axle needs new bearings or a ring-and-pinion, do that work concurrent with the locker installation."},{"id":"dana-44-rear-swap-cj7","title":"Dana 44 Rear Axle Swap \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"axles","subcategory":"rear-axle","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":12,"high":24},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":2400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","bearing press","angle grinder","welder (for perch relocation)","tape measure","dial indicator"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","axle setup experience","basic welding or fab shop access"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["axles","dana-44","rear-axle","swap","amc-20-replacement","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Currie Enterprises Dana 44 housing (custom CJ-7 width)","vendor":"Currie Enterprises","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"RuffStuff Dana 44 housing blank (CJ width)","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"1969-1979 Ford F-150 Spicer 44 donor axle","vendor":"Salvage/junkyard","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Yukon chromoly shaft set (donor D44 semi-float)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 44, 3.73 and up)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":720}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Dana 44 Rear Swap Guide \u2014 Pirate4x4","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/cj7-dana44-swap/"},{"title":"Axle Width and Track Width for Jeep CJ \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/cj-axle-widths/"}],"alternatives":["amc-20-problem-cj7","lockers-cj7"],"summary":"The Dana 44 rear swap is the definitive AMC 20 replacement on a purpose-built CJ-7 \u2014 but width matching to the CJ-7's 51.5-inch rear track is the constraint that determines your donor axle options.","description":"For most trail builds, the Superior Axle one-piece shaft conversion on the AMC 20 (see `amc-20-problem-cj7`) is adequate \u2014 it solves the shaft failure problem and lets you run a locker on the existing 8.875-inch ring gear housing. A Dana 44 swap makes sense when: (1) the AMC 20 housing is damaged or heavily worn, (2) you want a stronger ring gear for very large tire/locker builds on 35s or larger, or (3) you want the reassurance of a one-piece full-float or semi-float axle from the start.\n\nThe CJ-7 rear track width is approximately 51.5 inches (measured hub-face to hub-face). A replacement rear axle must match this \u2014 too wide or too narrow affects handling, tire clearance, and spare tire fitment. A JK Dana 44, for example, is too wide for the CJ-7 without spacers.\n\nThe classic donor axle is the 1969\u20131979 Ford F-150 with a Spicer 44 semi-float rear axle. In the correct year range, this axle measures close to the CJ-7's needed width and uses one-piece axle shafts (no C-clip problem). Junkyard sourcing runs $150\u2013200 for the housing.\n\nCurrie Enterprises and RuffStuff Specialties both build custom Dana 44 housing blanks specifically for CJ-7 applications at the correct width \u2014 more expensive ($680\u2013$1,100) but purpose-built with the correct spring perch spacing for the CJ-7 leaf spring mounts.\n\nWhatever donor axle you choose, the spring perches and shock mounts must be relocated to match the CJ-7 frame's spring mount spacing. This is welding work \u2014 either by you or a fab shop. The brake backing plates must also accommodate CJ-7 brake hardware (or be replaced with late-model hardware in a matching brake swap). Budget the fab work honestly \u2014 relocating perches and adapting brake hardware adds 4\u20138 hours of shop time."},{"id":"cj7-front-pads-rotors","title":"Front Disc Brake Service (Post-1977 CJ-7)","vehicle":"cj7","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":220},"tools_required":["3/4 in. socket and ratchet","13mm and 15mm wrenches","C-clamp or piston compressor","torque wrench","brake parts cleaner","wire brush","jack stands and floor jack","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","brake bleeding (if hose disturbed)"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1977-1986"],"tags":["brakes","front brakes","dana 30","rotors","pads","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Centric front brake rotor (1978.5-1986 CJ-7 Dana 30)","part_number":"121.66005","vendor":"Centric / O'Reilly","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Crown semi-metallic front pad set (1978-1981 two-bolt caliper)","part_number":"83300074","vendor":"Crown Automotive","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Crown semi-metallic front pad set (1982-1986 with titanium dust shield)","part_number":"5252631","vendor":"Crown Automotive","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Raybestos caliper guide pin and bushing kit","part_number":"H5004","vendor":"Raybestos","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Brake fluid DOT 3 (32 oz)","part_number":"\u2014","vendor":"Prestone / Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 CJ front brake parts 76-86","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-replacement-parts/jeep-cj5-cj7/cj-brakes.php"},{"title":"Torque King QU80301 rotor \u2014 1978.5-1985 CJ-7 Dana 30 spec","url":"https://torqueking.com/product/80301/qu80301-front-disc-brake-rotor-for-1978-5-1985-jeep-cj-7-dana-30/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Front disc brakes became standard on the CJ-7 in 1977. The Dana 30 with disc brakes uses a 10.5 in. rotor on a 5x5.5 bolt pattern, and the procedure is the same as any other vintage 4x4 disc swap \u2014 pads in 90 minutes, full rotor service in 3 hours.","description":"There are two distinct caliper generations to know about. 1976\u20131978 CJs used a six-bolt caliper bracket that bolts between the steering knuckle and the spindle. 1978.5\u20131986 went to a two-bolt caliper mount where the caliper bracket bolts directly to the knuckle. Year cutoff matters: order parts by VIN, not by model year, because the changeover was mid-1978.\n\nPads on the 1978\u20131981 trucks use a two-bolt caliper plate. 1982\u20131986 CJ-7s added a titanium dust shield behind the pad \u2014 same caliper, different pad backing plate. Crown Automotive part numbers reflect both versions; cross-check before you order.\n\nRotors on the Dana 30 are inexpensive ($55\u201380 in semi-metallic). Resurfacing an old rotor is rarely worth the labor cost. If the rotor has lip wear deeper than the thickness of a quarter, it's past minimum spec \u2014 replace it. Minimum thickness is stamped on the rotor hat; if you can't read it, measure with calipers and compare to the new rotor.\n\nThe caliper itself is a single-piston floating design. Sticking guide pins are the most common cause of uneven pad wear and pulling. When the caliper is off, slide the guide pins out and inspect: the rubber bushings should be supple, not cracked. The Raybestos H5004 kit replaces both pins and bushings for under $20 and prevents a return trip in six months.\n\nIf the caliper piston is seized, replace the caliper outright. A rebuild kit is cheaper, but a 40-year-old caliper body often has pitting that defeats a new seal. Loaded calipers from Centric or Raybestos run $60\u2013100 each.\n\n**Safety pillar applies here.** Test the pedal before the first drive. Pump until firm. Drive 50 feet, brake gently. Then drive 200 feet, brake harder. If the pedal feels spongy, you have air in the line \u2014 bleed before you leave the driveway.","steps":["Loosen the front lug nuts a half-turn. Jack the front of the Jeep and set jack stands under the frame. Pull the wheels.","Spray the back of the caliper with brake parts cleaner to knock loose any debris that could spray when you crack fittings.","Pull the two caliper slide bolts (15mm on most years). Slide the caliper off the rotor. Hang it from the spring or coil with a wire \u2014 do not let it dangle from the brake hose.","Pry the old pads out of the bracket. Note their position; the outer pad has a wear sensor clip on some years.","Inspect the slide pins. If gritty or dry, pull them, clean them, and re-grease with high-temp brake caliper grease (or replace with the Raybestos kit).","Pull the rotor straight off the studs. If rust-locked, hit it with penetrant, wait 10 minutes, then strike the rotor face (not the hub) with a deadblow hammer.","Wire-brush the hub face clean. A rusty hub face is the #1 cause of brake pulsation on a fresh install.","Install the new rotor. Hand-thread two lug nuts to hold it flat against the hub.","Compress the caliper piston with a C-clamp or piston compressor. Open the bleeder on the caliper as you compress \u2014 pushing dirty fluid back up the line into the master cylinder is bad practice. Catch fluid in a rag.","Drop the new pads into the bracket. Reinstall the caliper over the new rotor. Torque caliper slide bolts to 25\u201335 ft-lb.","Wheels back on. Lug nut torque: 80 ft-lb on a 5x5.5 pattern.","Pump the brake pedal until firm before driving. Top off the master cylinder reservoir.","Bed the pads: 5 stops from 30 mph to 5 mph with moderate pressure, then 3 stops from 50 to 10 mph with firmer pressure. Don't lock the wheels."],"pros":["Massive safety improvement over neglected brakes \u2014 the single highest-leverage maintenance you can do","All parts are available, cheap, and shipping fast \u2014 no need to wait","Doable in an afternoon with hand tools"],"cons":["Year cutoff at mid-1978 is real \u2014 confirm caliper type by VIN, not model year","If you have to push fluid back into the master cylinder, contamination risk goes up; open the bleeder while compressing","Old hardware (slide pins, clips, dust boots) is usually past its service life \u2014 budget for the full hardware kit, not pads"]},{"id":"dana-300-tcase-cj7","title":"Dana 300 Transfer Case \u2014 The Upgrade to Know About \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"transfer-case","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":950},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","transmission jack","torque wrench","snap ring pliers","bearing driver set","drain pans"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1980-1986"],"tags":["drivetrain","transfer-case","dana-300","dana-20","crawl-ratio","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Dana 300 rebuilt unit (from Renegade/Laredo/Scrambler donor)","vendor":"Salvage/rebuilt","price_estimate_usd":400},{"name":"Dana 300 rebuild kit (bearings, seals, gaskets)","vendor":"Novak Conversions","price_estimate_usd":115},{"name":"Dana 300 SYE (slip yoke eliminator) kit","vendor":"Novak Conversions","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"Advance Adapters Dana 300 drop kit","vendor":"Advance Adapters","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Dana 300 Transfer Case Guide \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/dana300/"},{"title":"Dana 300 vs Dana 20 Crawl Ratio Comparison \u2014 Pirate4x4","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/d300-vs-d20/"}],"alternatives":["lockers-cj7","regear-cj7"],"summary":"The Dana 300 is one of the best transfer cases ever put in a Jeep \u2014 its 2.61:1 low range delivers noticeably better crawl ratio than the Dana 20's 2.03:1, and it's a direct bolt-in swap if the transmission bolt pattern matches.","description":"The Dana 20 transfer case was the base transfer case on most CJ-7s \u2014 it has a 2.03:1 low range reduction. The Dana 300 was factory equipment on CJ-7 Renegade, Laredo, and some other trim-level builds, as well as the Scrambler (CJ-8). It has a 2.61:1 low range.\n\nThe crawl ratio improvement is significant: with 4.10 axle gears, a D20 CJ-7 has an overall low-range crawl ratio of approximately 40:1. The same rig with a Dana 300 has roughly 52:1. This 30% improvement in mechanical advantage is noticeable on steep climbs and precision rock work \u2014 the engine doesn't have to work as hard, the rig moves more slowly for a given throttle input, and recovery from wheelie situations is easier.\n\nFor a CJ-7 built for rock crawling, the Dana 300 is the right transfer case. If you have a D20, the D300 swap is a legitimate upgrade.\n\nThe Dana 300 is a direct bolt-in if your CJ-7 has the same transmission bolt pattern as the T-4, T-5, or T-176 manual transmissions paired with the D300 from the factory. The D300 mounts to the back of the transmission with a direct bolt pattern \u2014 no adapter plate needed if the patterns match. Confirm the specific pattern for your transmission before sourcing a D300.\n\nIf your CJ-7 has an automatic transmission (TH400 or TH904), an adapter may be required. Advance Adapters makes a D300 drop kit ($280) for this application.\n\nThe best sources for a used Dana 300 are: Renegade and Laredo CJ-7s at salvage yards, Scrambler (CJ-8) donors, and the early Wrangler YJ (1987\u20131995 YJ used the D300 in some configurations). A rebuilt unit runs $350\u2013450 in good condition. Inspect the unit before buying \u2014 check the output shaft bearings for play and look for stripped threads on the shift detent plugs, which are a common neglect point on used D300s.\n\nLike the Dana 20, the Dana 300's slip yoke rear output creates driveshaft angle problems at lift heights above 3\u20134 inches. The Novak SYE kit ($340) converts the D300 output to a fixed flange for a CV rear driveshaft. If your D300 CJ-7 has a lift and you're experiencing rear driveshaft vibration, the SYE is the fix."},{"id":"lockers-cj7","title":"Locker Selection \u2014 Front and Rear \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","bearing press","dial indicator"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","differential service experience"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["drivetrain","lockers","traction","amc-20","dana-30","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right (Dana 30 front)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (Dana 30 front)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"Superior Axle one-piece shaft conversion kit (AMC 20)","vendor":"Superior Axle & Gear","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Powertrax Lock-Right (AMC 20 rear \u2014 after one-piece conversion)","vendor":"Powertrax","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"ARB Air Locker (AMC 20 rear \u2014 after one-piece conversion)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":720}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Locker and Axle Upgrade Guide \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/cj7-lockers/"},{"title":"AMC 20 One-Piece Conversion Before Locker \u2014 Superior Axle","url":"https://www.superioraxle.com/amc20-conversion/"}],"alternatives":["amc-20-problem-cj7","dana-30-front-cj7","roll-cage-cj7"],"summary":"Before you buy a rear locker for a CJ-7, buy the AMC 20 one-piece shaft conversion. This is not optional \u2014 a rear locker on stock two-piece C-clip AMC 20 shafts is a documented trail disaster.","description":"The CJ-7's locker build sequence differs from the CJ-5 because of the AMC 20 problem. On a CJ-7 with a stock AMC 20 rear axle, the order of operations is:\n\n1. Convert the AMC 20 to one-piece chromoly shafts (Superior Axle kit, $420) \u2014 see `amc-20-problem-cj7` for the full explanation\n2. Install the rear locker into the converted AMC 20\n3. Upgrade front Dana 30 shafts to chromoly\n4. Install the front locker\n5. Verify cage or ROPS is in place before driving the locked rig on technical terrain\n\nSkipping step 1 and going straight to the rear locker is the mistake that leaves CJ-7s sitting on the trail with a missing rear wheel. The extra $420 for the shaft conversion is not negotiable on a built rig.\n\nWith one-piece shafts installed, the AMC 20 supports the Powertrax Lock-Right ($280) or the ARB Air Locker ($720). The Lock-Right installs in the existing carrier (with the Auburn open carrier replacement required for the one-piece shaft design \u2014 typically included in the conversion kit). The ARB requires its own carrier and an air system.\n\nThe ARB Air Locker is worth the premium on a CJ-7 that sees both pavement and trail use. The selectable engagement makes pavement driving comfortable \u2014 unlocked for normal driving, locked on demand for trail sections. The Lock-Right's ratcheting behavior on pavement is tolerable but not preferable for a rig used both on and off road.\n\nCJ-7s equipped with the Dana 300 transfer case (Renegade, Laredo, and some late production) have a 2.61:1 low range vs. the Dana 20's 2.03:1. This means the Dana 300 CJ-7 has a significantly better crawl ratio and puts less torque demand on the axles in rock crawling. If your CJ-7 has a D300, the lockers are working against a more favorable gear ratio \u2014 slightly less stress on shafts and gears compared to a D20 CJ-7 under the same conditions."},{"id":"regear-cj7","title":"Re-Gearing for Bigger Tires \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"gearing","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","bearing press","dial indicator","ring gear holder","pinion depth tool","marking compound"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","differential setup experience \u2014 or take it to a shop"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["drivetrain","gearing","regear","amc-20","dana-30","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon 4.10 ring-and-pinion (Dana 30)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Yukon 4.10 ring-and-pinion (AMC 20)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":215},{"name":"Motive Gear 4.56 set (Dana 30)","vendor":"Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":185},{"name":"Motive Gear 4.56 set (AMC 20)","vendor":"Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Dana 30 master install kit","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":85},{"name":"AMC 20 master install kit","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":110}],"citations":[{"title":"Gear Ratio Calculator \u2014 Randy's Ring & Pinion","url":"https://www.ringpinion.com/gear-ratio-calculator/"},{"title":"CJ-7 Re-Gear Tech \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/cj7-gearing/"}],"alternatives":["lockers-cj7","amc-20-problem-cj7","dana-300-tcase-cj7"],"summary":"Factory CJ-7 gears (3.54 or 3.73) were matched to 30-inch tires. Going to 33s or 35s without re-gearing significantly undercuts the AMC 258's modest torque output and worsens crawl ratio.","description":"The AMC 258 inline-six is the most common CJ-7 engine \u2014 and it produces approximately 110\u2013120 hp and 210 lb-ft of torque depending on year and tune. This is not a powerful engine. The factory gear ratios and 30-inch tires were calibrated to work with that power output. When you increase tire diameter by 10% (30s to 33s), you effectively lengthen the final drive ratio by the same proportion \u2014 the engine has to work harder to move the vehicle at any given rpm.\n\nOn a CJ-7 with the AMC 258, re-gearing is more important than on a more powerful engine. Under-gearing this engine produces sluggish acceleration, excessive heat in the drivetrain, and poor crawl ratio for technical trail work.\n\n| Tire Size | Recommended Ratio | Notes |\n|-----------|------------------|-------|\n| 30x9.5 (stock) | 3.54 or 3.73 | factory spec |\n| 31x10.5 | 3.73 | livable, modest penalty |\n| 33x12.5 | 4.10 | correct for AMC 258 |\n| 35x12.5 | 4.56 | full cage required at this size |\n\nBoth the Dana 30 front and the AMC 20 rear (or D44 if swapped) must be re-geared to match. Running mismatched ratios in 4WD causes binding and driveline stress.\n\nThe AMC 20 uses an unusual ring gear pitch that is not shared with other common axles. Cheap imported ring-and-pinion sets for the AMC 20 have a documented quality problem \u2014 the gear tooth profile is sometimes inaccurate, leading to early failure and noise. Use Yukon Gear ($215) or Motive Gear ($200) for the AMC 20 rear \u2014 these manufacturers produce AMC 20 gears that are accurately machined to spec. Avoid no-name or unbranded AMC 20 gears regardless of price.\n\nAs with the CJ-5, differential setup is precision work. If you haven't set ring-and-pinion backlash and bearing preload before, have a shop do the actual gear installation. Budget $300\u2013500 per axle for labor."},{"id":"battery-alternator-cj7","title":"Battery and Alternator Upgrade \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"electrical","subcategory":"electrical-system","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":580},"tools_required":["socket set","multimeter","wire crimper","battery terminal brush"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","basic electrical"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["electrical","battery","alternator","charging-system","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Optima RedTop 34/78 battery","vendor":"Optima Batteries","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Odyssey PC925 battery","vendor":"Odyssey Battery","price_estimate_usd":215},{"name":"Powermaster 94A replacement alternator (AMC 258)","vendor":"Powermaster","price_estimate_usd":235},{"name":"NAPA rebuilt 94A alternator (AMC 258)","vendor":"NAPA","price_estimate_usd":145}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Alternator Upgrade Guide \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/cj7-alternator/"},{"title":"Powermaster Alternator Application Guide","url":"https://www.powermastermotorsports.com/jeep-cj-alternator/"}],"alternatives":["wiring-harness-cj7","winch-cj7"],"summary":"The factory CJ-7 alternator produces 60 amps \u2014 fine for a stock rig with a radio, but inadequate the moment you add a winch, auxiliary lighting, or any serious accessories.","description":"The AMC-era CJ-7 came with a 60-amp alternator. On a stock rig used primarily on the highway, this is sufficient. Once you start adding electrical accessories \u2014 winch, LED light bar, auxiliary lights, phone chargers, CB, GPS, compressor \u2014 the 60A alternator can no longer meet peak demand. The battery compensates by discharging to make up the difference. Over time, this cycle-drains the battery and reduces its life.\n\nSpecifically, a winch at full load draws 250\u2013400 amps. The battery must supply the bulk of this current since no alternator keeps up with winch demand. But after the winch pull, the alternator must recharge the battery. A 60A alternator takes a long time to recover from a heavy winch use. A 94A or 110A alternator recovers faster and handles the ongoing electrical load of lights and accessories without draining the battery.\n\nPowermaster makes a direct-fit 94-amp replacement alternator for the AMC 258 ($235) \u2014 same bolt pattern as the factory unit, higher output, new-manufacture quality. This is the clean upgrade for a CJ-7 with added accessories. NAPA's rebuilt 94A alternator ($145) is the budget option using a reman core; quality depends on the shop that rebuilt it.\n\nThe upgrade is straightforward: unbolt the factory unit, install the new alternator, reconnect the same wires (the output terminal and the field connector are the same), re-tension the belt. An ammeter check with a multimeter confirms the new alternator is producing its rated output (should show 13.8\u201314.4 volts at the battery at idle with accessories on).\n\nPair the alternator upgrade with an Optima RedTop ($190) or Odyssey PC925 ($215) AGM battery. Both are vibration-resistant and better suited to the cyclic charging that winch use creates. The Optima RedTop is the more popular choice for engine-bay installation in a CJ-7; the Odyssey PC925 is a better choice if you plan to use the winch heavily."},{"id":"wiring-harness-cj7","title":"Wiring Harness Replacement \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"electrical","subcategory":"wiring","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":680},"tools_required":["wire stripper/crimper","multimeter","heat gun","electrical tape","zip ties","terminal puller","label maker or tape"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","basic electrical \u2014 reading wiring diagrams"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["electrical","wiring","harness","painless","hei","fire-safety","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Painless Performance 18-circuit universal harness","vendor":"Painless Performance","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Omix-ADA CJ-7 replacement wiring harness (year-specific)","vendor":"Omix-ADA","price_estimate_usd":295},{"name":"Delco HEI distributor (AMC 258 conversion)","vendor":"Summit Racing","price_estimate_usd":85},{"name":"Weatherpack connector assortment","vendor":"Molex / WiringPro","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Painless Performance CJ-7 Harness Installation Guide","url":"https://www.painlessperformance.com/instructions/cj7/"},{"title":"CJ-7 HEI Conversion Tech \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/cj7-hei/"}],"alternatives":["battery-alternator-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7's factory wiring is a 40-year-old system with cracked insulation, bullet connectors corroded to powder, and glass fuses in a fuse block that may itself be compromised \u2014 rewiring it is one of the most reliability-improving modifications you can make.","description":"The factory AMC wiring harness from 1976\u20131986 uses rubber-insulated wire that ages poorly. The insulation cracks, especially where the harness routes through metal firewall grommets and along vibrating frame rails. The bullet connectors \u2014 the factory connection standard throughout the CJ-7 \u2014 corrode in the presence of moisture and road salt and eventually lose continuity. This produces the classic CJ-7 electrical gremlins: gauges that work intermittently, brake lights that don't illuminate, an engine that cuts out in rain.\n\nMany CJ-7s have accumulated decades of owner modifications \u2014 added lighting, stereos, CB radios, GPS units \u2014 wired with direct crimps and tape over the factory harness, creating diagnostic layers that make tracing a problem nearly impossible. A complete rewire with a quality harness resolves this permanently.\n\nThe Omix-ADA year-specific CJ-7 harness ($295) is the direct replacement choice \u2014 factory routing, factory colors, factory connector locations. If your CJ-7 is stock or near-stock, this is the clear path. Order the year-specific kit; the CJ-7 harness changed across the production run.\n\nThe Painless Performance 18-circuit universal harness ($420) is the right choice for a modified CJ-7 \u2014 different engine, added circuits for accessories, or a non-stock fusebox location. Painless harnesses use labeled wires, modern blade fuses, and include a complete installation manual. Installation takes two full days but produces a system with clear documentation you can troubleshoot 20 years from now.\n\nIf your CJ-7 still has points ignition, the wiring replacement is the right time to convert to a Delco HEI electronic distributor. The HEI for the AMC 258 requires one wire \u2014 12V ignition power \u2014 simplifying the ignition wiring significantly. The distributor runs about $85 new, the conversion eliminates tune-up intervals, and the HEI improves starting reliability and fuel economy. Converting the ignition to HEI at the same time as the harness replacement means the new harness is wired for the correct ignition system from the start."},{"id":"roll-cage-cj7","title":"Roll Cage for the CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"interior","subcategory":"safety","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":60},"cost_usd":{"low":900,"high":3500},"tools_required":["MIG welder","angle grinder","notcher or cope saw","tube bender","drill press","measuring tape","level"],"skills_required":["welding","advanced fabrication \u2014 or hire a professional fabricator"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["safety","roll-cage","rops","interior","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Poison Spyder CJ-7 cage kit (pre-bent)","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"GenRight Off Road CJ-7 cage kit","vendor":"GenRight Off Road","price_estimate_usd":950},{"name":"TNT Custom Steel CJ-7 ROPS bolt-in","vendor":"TNT Custom Steel","price_estimate_usd":880},{"name":"DOM 1.75\" x 0.120\" tubing (20 ft)","vendor":"Metal Supermarkets","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Cage floor plate anchors (set of 4)","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Roll Cage Options \u2014 GenRight Off Road","url":"https://www.genright.com/cj7-roll-cage"},{"title":"Poison Spyder CJ-7 Cage Kit Installation Notes","url":"https://www.poisonspyder.com/cj7-cage-install"}],"alternatives":["lift-springs-cj7","soa-cj7","tire-sizing-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7 left the factory without a roll cage. It is slightly more stable than the CJ-5 due to the longer wheelbase, but it still rolls under the wrong conditions \u2014 and without a cage, a rollover is potentially fatal.","description":"Like the CJ-5, the CJ-7 was manufactured without any roll protection. The optional factory soft top and later hardtop provided no structural protection in a rollover. The CJ-7's 93.5-inch wheelbase gives it more rollover resistance than the CJ-5 in equivalent conditions, but \"more stable than a CJ-5\" is a relative measure that does not mean \"safe to roll without a cage.\"\n\nA lifted CJ-7 running 33s with lockers on off-camber technical terrain is in rollover territory. The longer wheelbase helps, but the physics of a tall, narrow rig on steep off-camber ground eventually overcome the wheelbase advantage. Drivers have been seriously injured in rolled CJ-7s without roll protection. The cage is the modification that changes a potentially fatal event into a survivable one.\n\nThe CJ-7 has better factory cage kit support than the CJ-5 because of its larger production numbers and ongoing popularity. Poison Spyder, GenRight Off Road, and TNT Custom Steel all make pre-bent cage kits specifically for the CJ-7.\n\nPoison Spyder's CJ-7 kit ($1,100) is well-regarded for fitment accuracy \u2014 the pre-bent tubes reduce fabrication time and improve the final geometry. The kit requires welding for final installation; it is not a bolt-in. GenRight's kit ($950) is comparable quality with slightly different geometry. TNT Custom Steel's ROPS bolt-in ($880) is the option for a builder without access to a welder \u2014 it installs with grade-8 hardware into floor-mounted plates without welding.\n\nA basic ROPS \u2014 a main hoop behind the seats with floor plate anchors \u2014 is the minimum acceptable protection for a CJ-7 with any lift. TNT's bolt-in ROPS provides this. It protects against a direct sideways roll but offers less protection in a tumble. For a CJ-7 doing more than mild trail work, a full cage is the right standard.\n\nIf you are building an SOA CJ-7 (see `soa-cj7`), the cage must be built before or concurrent with the SOA lift. An SOA CJ-7 without a cage is a rig with 4\u20136 inches of additional lift, a significantly raised center of gravity, and no protection if it goes over. The cage and SOA are inseparable parts of a serious build \u2014 not a staged sequence where the cage comes later."},{"id":"cj7-coolant-flush","title":"Coolant Flush \u2014 AMC Sixes and V8s","vehicle":"cj7","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":60},"tools_required":["drain pan (4 gal minimum)","garden hose","screwdriver (for hose clamps)","channel lock pliers","funnel","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["coolant","cooling system","flush","amc 258","amc 304","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Prestone DEX-COOL 50/50 Premixed Antifreeze (universal green-compatible)","part_number":"AF2100","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Stant 195\u00b0F thermostat (AMC 258 / 304)","part_number":"13559","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Gates upper radiator hose (CJ-7 258)","part_number":"21484","vendor":"Gates","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Distilled water (mix at 50/50 with concentrate)","part_number":"\u2014","vendor":"any grocery store","price_estimate_usd":4}],"citations":[{"title":"AMC 258 / 304 cooling system capacity discussion","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f12/cooling-system-capacity-possible-flush-447909/"},{"title":"Novak cooling guide for Jeeps","url":"http://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/cooling"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Both AMC engines in the CJ-7 hold around 11 quarts (258) to 14 quarts (304) of coolant total \u2014 and the radiator drain only pulls about half of that. To do a real flush, you have to drain the engine block too.","description":"The mistake most owners make is opening the radiator petcock, letting two gallons run out, refilling, and calling it done. The radiator on a CJ holds maybe 5 quarts. The rest sits in the engine block, the heater core, and the hoses. If you only drain the radiator, you're diluting old coolant with new \u2014 not flushing.\n\nA real flush means opening the block drains on both sides of the engine (under the exhaust manifolds, straightforward to miss) or pulling the lower radiator hose. With the block open, you can run a garden hose into the radiator filler neck and watch the rusty water come out the bottom until it runs clear. Then you close everything up and refill with 50/50 distilled water and concentrated antifreeze.\n\nCoolant chemistry on these engines is forgiving. Stock spec was green ethylene glycol. Universal \"compatible with any color\" formulations (Prestone AF2100, Peak Long Life) are fine. Avoid mixing Dex-Cool (orange, GM) with green in the same system \u2014 the byproducts can clog the heater core. If you're not sure what's in there, drain everything and start with one type.\n\nThermostat: stock is 195\u00b0F. If your engine runs hot in summer Phoenix-style heat, a 180\u00b0F thermostat (Stant 13558) buys 15\u00b0F of headroom \u2014 but a thermostat doesn't fix a clogged radiator or a worn water pump. Diagnose the cause first.\n\nDistilled water matters. Tap water leaves mineral deposits in the radiator core that build up over years. If you're spending $40 on antifreeze, spend $4 on distilled water \u2014 don't undo the work.","steps":["Engine cold. If the radiator is even warm to the touch, wait. Pressurized coolant against your face is the worst kind of preventable injury.","Place the drain pan under the radiator. Locate the petcock \u2014 driver-side bottom of the radiator on most CJ-7s.","Remove the radiator cap (cold engine only). Open the petcock counterclockwise. Let the radiator drain fully \u2014 about 5 quarts.","Locate the block drain plugs. On the AMC 258 they're on the driver side of the block, below the exhaust manifold (one plug, often a 3/8 in. square head). On the AMC 304 there are two \u2014 one each side. Use a wrench, not pliers; the heads round off easily.","Open each block drain. Catch the coolant. Plan on another 5\u20136 quarts coming out of the block.","If the coolant is brown/rusty: close the block drains, close the petcock, fill the radiator with distilled water only, run the engine to operating temp (heater on full hot), shut down, let cool, drain again. Repeat until water runs clear.","Pull the lower radiator hose for a final rinse if you want to be thorough \u2014 that's the only way to see actual flow direction.","Close all drains and reinstall the lower hose with fresh clamps.","Refill: 50% antifreeze concentrate, 50% distilled water, until the radiator is full and the overflow bottle reads \"cold.\"","Start the engine with the cap off. Let it idle until the thermostat opens \u2014 you'll see the coolant level drop as air burps out. Top off the radiator. Replace the cap.","Drive 15 minutes. Let cool. Top off the overflow bottle to the cold line.","Recheck after one week of driving."],"pros":["Cheapest preventive maintenance against radiator and heater core failure","3-year / 50,000-mile interval is the standard; sooner if towing or seeing high desert temps","A clean cooling system runs 10\u201315\u00b0F cooler than a 5-year-old neglected one"],"cons":["Block drain plugs are often seized or hidden behind exhaust manifolds \u2014 budget extra time for a first-time flush","Used coolant is toxic to pets; capture all of it and recycle at any auto parts store","A flush won't fix a clogged radiator, a worn water pump, or a failing thermostat \u2014 diagnose, then flush"]},{"id":"cj7-differential-fluid","title":"Differential Fluid \u2014 Dana 30 Front, AMC 20 Rear","vehicle":"cj7","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.75,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":50},"tools_required":["3/8 in. ratchet (square drive into fill plug)","13/16 in. or 3/4 in. socket","drain pan","fluid pump or gear oil squeeze bottle","shop rags","RTV gasket sealant (if dropping the cover)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["differential","gear oil","dana 30","amc 20","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline SynPower 80W-90 GL-5 gear oil (1 qt) \u2014 open diff","part_number":"VV833","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Royal Purple Max-Gear 75W-90 (1 qt) \u2014 limited-slip safe","part_number":"01300","vendor":"Royal Purple","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Mopar friction modifier (for Trac-Lok limited slip)","part_number":"4318060AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Permatex Ultra Black RTV (if pulling the cover)","part_number":"82180","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":10}],"citations":[{"title":"ExtremeTerrain \u2014 Jeep CJ fluid capacities","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/jeep-cj-fluid-capacities-and-type-tech-guide.html"},{"title":"AMC 20 fluid fill discussion","url":"https://www.cj-8.com/threads/amc-20-fill.53049/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Dana 30 front takes 1.25 quarts of 80W-90 GL-5. The AMC 20 rear takes 2.4 quarts of the same. Both have a fill plug and a drain plug \u2014 no cover to drop unless you want to inspect the gears or run a full clean.","description":"This is the maintenance item that gets skipped longest because nothing visible signals that the fluid is dead. By the time the diff is whining, the gear faces are already pitted. The standard service interval is every 30,000 miles for daily-driver use, every 12,000 miles if you wheel hard, and immediately after any water crossing deep enough to submerge the diff cover. Water in gear oil turns it the color of coffee with cream \u2014 if you see that on the dipstick or fill-plug threads, change it now.\n\nBoth axles use the same 80W-90 GL-5 gear oil for open-diff applications. If your CJ-7 has the Trac-Lok limited-slip option in the rear, you also need a friction modifier \u2014 without it, the clutch packs will chatter on tight turns. Mopar friction modifier is the factory spec; one bottle treats one diff. Synthetic 80W-90 (Valvoline SynPower, Mobil 1 75W-90) is a worthwhile upgrade \u2014 better cold-weather pumping and slower oxidation.\n\nDrain and fill plugs on the Dana 30 are both 3/8 in. square drive \u2014 you put the ratchet end directly into the plug, no socket needed. The fill plug is on the front face of the diff (between the pinion and the cover); the drain plug is below it. Old plugs can be tight. If you can't break the fill plug loose first, don't drain \u2014 you'll be stranded with an empty axle and no way to refill.\n\nThe AMC 20 rear uses the same 3/8 in. square drive on the fill plug. It's the differential many owners love to upgrade \u2014 the two-piece rear axle shaft is the well-known weak point, but the ring gear and diff itself are stout. Fluid change is the same procedure as the Dana 30, more volume.\n\n**The \"loosen the fill plug first\" rule is absolute.** Crack the fill plug before the drain plug, every time. Same logic as oil pan plug + filter \u2014 if the fill won't open, the drain stays closed.","steps":["Drive the Jeep for 5 minutes to warm the gear oil. Warm oil drains faster and carries more contamination.","Park on level ground. Chock the rear wheels. Set the parking brake.","Wire-brush both plugs on the diff you're servicing \u2014 debris in the threads becomes debris in the diff.","Break the fill plug loose first. If it won't move with a 3/8 in. ratchet, hit it with penetrant, wait 10 minutes, try again. If it still won't move, do not drain.","Once the fill plug is loose, place the drain pan under the drain plug. Pull the drain plug. Let the gear oil drain fully \u2014 about 10 minutes.","Inspect the drained fluid. Metal sparkle from break-in (on a new gear set) is normal. Larger chunks or a milky color are not \u2014 diagnose before refilling.","Reinstall the drain plug. Torque to 24 ft-lb.","Pump fresh gear oil through the fill hole until it starts to overflow back out \u2014 that's the correct level on any \"fill to the hole\" axle. Dana 30: ~1.25 qt. AMC 20: ~2.4 qt.","If running a Trac-Lok limited slip in the rear, add 4 oz of Mopar friction modifier (or equivalent) before the final fill.","Reinstall the fill plug. Torque to 24 ft-lb.","Wipe the diff clean. Drive a few miles and check for weep from either plug."],"pros":["Cheapest preventive maintenance against a $1,500+ ring-and-pinion replacement","Plug-and-fill design \u2014 no cover gasket, no RTV, no overnight cure time","Same fluid for both axles streamlines the parts list"],"cons":["Old plugs can be seized \u2014 crack the fill before the drain or you'll regret it","If you crossed water and the fluid is milky, you need to pull the cover and inspect, not refill","Trac-Lok diffs require friction modifier; missing this step makes the diff chatter in turns"]},{"id":"cj7-oil-change","title":"Oil Change \u2014 AMC 258 I6 and AMC 304 V8","vehicle":"cj7","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":50},"tools_required":["13mm socket or 1/2 in. socket","ratchet","oil filter wrench (3 3/8 in. cap or band)","drain pan (6 qt minimum)","funnel","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["oil change","amc 258","amc 304","maintenance","zddp"],"parts":[{"name":"Wix 51258 oil filter (AMC 258 I6)","part_number":"51258","vendor":"Wix / NAPA Gold 1258","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Wix 51515 oil filter (AMC 304 V8)","part_number":"51515","vendor":"Wix / NAPA Gold 1515","price_estimate_usd":11},{"name":"Shell Rotella T4 15W-40 (high-ZDDP for flat-tappet cam)","part_number":"550045126","vendor":"Shell","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Valvoline VR1 Racing 10W-30 (high-ZDDP, alternative)","part_number":"VV205","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":32}],"citations":[{"title":"Wix 51258 \u2014 AMC 258 application","url":"https://www.summitracing.com/search/brand/wix-filters/part-type/oil-filters/make/jeep/model/cj7/engine-size/4-2l-258"},{"title":"AMC 304 oil capacity and weight discussion","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f8/304-oil-capacity-900334/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Both AMC engines in the CJ-7 take five quarts of 10W-30 or 15W-40, and both want a high-zinc (ZDDP) oil because the cam and lifters are flat-tappet \u2014 modern passenger car oils don't carry enough zinc to protect them.","description":"The 258 inline-six and the 304 V8 share the same service approach. Drain pan under the pan, pull the drain plug, swap the filter, refill with five quarts. Where DIYers get into trouble is oil selection, not procedure: modern API SP oils have ZDDP capped at ~800 ppm to protect catalytic converters in newer engines. That cap is fine for a roller-cam motor and a problem for a flat-tappet AMC. Cam wipe is the failure mode \u2014 and it shows up as a flat lobe a season later, not the day you change the oil.\n\nThe fix is to run a high-ZDDP oil. Diesel-rated 15W-40 (Shell Rotella T4) is the cheap path and still carries ~1,200 ppm zinc. Valvoline VR1 Racing 10W-30 carries more (~1,400 ppm) and costs more. Either is correct. Avoid generic \"high-mileage\" oils \u2014 they're high in detergent, not zinc.\n\nFilter sizing matters on the 258 specifically. The long-bodied Wix 51258 sits close to the driver-side engine mount. If the mounts are soft (and on a 40-year-old Jeep they often are), high drivetrain torque can let the engine flex enough to tag the filter. Inspect the mounts before you commit to a long filter. If they look cooked, install fresh mounts or run the shorter Wix 51522 \u2014 same threading, shorter can, more clearance.\n\nThe 304 V8 takes the Wix 51515 \u2014 a different filter entirely. Don't cross them.\n\nTotal capacity for both engines with a new filter is five quarts. Some shops recommend 5.5 quarts (\"five plus a half for the filter\") which lands the dipstick a hair above the full mark \u2014 fine, but the official spec is five.","steps":["Run the engine for 5 minutes to warm the oil and suspend the sludge.","Park on level ground with the front wheels chocked. Set the parking brake.","Slide the drain pan under the pan plug (passenger-side rear corner on the 258, sump bottom on the 304).","Pull the 13mm or 1/2 in. drain plug. Let it drain for at least 10 minutes \u2014 drips matter.","Position the pan under the filter (driver-side block, mid-engine). Spin the filter off with a cap or band wrench.","Wipe the filter mounting surface clean. Pre-fill the new filter with fresh oil. Smear oil on the new gasket.","Thread the new filter on by hand. When the gasket touches the block, snug it 3/4 turn \u2014 no wrench.","Reinstall the drain plug with the crush washer (replace if flattened). Torque: 25 ft-lb on both engines.","Add five quarts of high-ZDDP oil. Start the engine. Watch for the oil pressure light to drop within 5 seconds.","Check for leaks at the filter and drain plug. Shut down. Recheck the dipstick after 5 minutes of settling."],"pros":["Cheapest and highest-leverage maintenance on either AMC engine","Done in 30 minutes once you have the routine","Keeps the flat-tappet cam alive \u2014 every change matters"],"cons":["Filter clearance to the driver-side motor mount on the 258 is tight; soft mounts compound the problem","ZDDP requirement narrows your oil shelf at most parts stores \u2014 diesel section or specialty racing oils only"]},{"id":"cj7-spark-plugs","title":"Spark Plugs \u2014 AMC 258 and 304","vehicle":"cj7","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":35},"tools_required":["5/8 in. spark plug socket","3/8 in. ratchet","ratchet extension (6 in.)","torque wrench (in-lb or low-range ft-lb)","spark plug gap tool or feeler gauge","anti-seize compound"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["spark plugs","amc 258","amc 304","ignition","tune-up"],"parts":[{"name":"Champion RN12YC copper plug (AMC 258 stock)","part_number":"RN12YC / 404","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":4},{"name":"Champion RN12YC copper plug (AMC 304 stock)","part_number":"RN12YC / 404","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":4},{"name":"NGK BPR5ES (cross-reference)","part_number":"BPR5ES","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":4},{"name":"Permatex anti-seize compound","part_number":"81343","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":7}],"citations":[{"title":"Champion RN12YC 0.035 gap spec","url":"https://www.sparkplugs.com/champion-404-rn12yc-nickel-spark-plug"},{"title":"Jeep CJ spark plug specifications","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/jeep-cj-spark-plug-specs-tech-guide.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Both the AMC 258 and 304 use Champion RN12YC (or equivalent) at 0.035 in. gap and 26\u201330 ft-lb torque into cast iron heads. Anti-seize is optional but recommended on a 40-year-old aluminum-free engine that has seen seasonal humidity.","description":"This is the second-cheapest tune-up you can do, and the one that most often gets skipped because the engine \"still runs.\" The 258 will tolerate worn plugs for tens of thousands of miles before it stumbles. By the time it stumbles, the cap, rotor, and wires are probably also tired. Fresh plugs every 20,000 miles on a daily-driver CJ-7 is the standard interval; every 5,000 if it sees serious dust or short trips.\n\nThe 258 firing order is **1-5-3-6-2-4** (front to back: cylinders 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6). The 304 V8 is **1-8-4-3-6-5-7-2** \u2014 passenger bank is 1, 3, 5, 7 front to back; driver bank is 2, 4, 6, 8 front to back. Pull and label one wire at a time so you don't have to relearn the order. If wires are cracked, ceramic insulators are oil-stained, or the boots feel hard, replace the whole set. NAPA Belden or Accel 5mm wires are the standard upgrade.\n\nStock plug for both engines is the Champion RN12YC copper. The \"RN\" prefix means resistor, nominal heat range; the \"12Y\" is the heat number; the \"C\" denotes copper core. NGK BPR5ES is the direct cross-reference and many owners prefer it for a sharper spark in worn engines. Avoid platinum or iridium plugs unless your ignition is upgraded \u2014 the stock points-or-Motorcraft ignition doesn't put out the voltage to clean a fine-tip plug.\n\nGap is **0.035 in.** on both engines. The factory tolerance is 0.033\u20130.038 in. The 258 with HEI distributor (1978+ on most Jeeps) can run 0.040 in. if the coil is healthy, but stay at 0.035 if you've got original ignition.","steps":["Let the engine cool completely. Aluminum-thread strip is not the concern (these heads are cast iron) but a hot plug can seize against carbon.","Blow or brush debris out of each plug well before you crack any plug loose. Dirt that falls into the cylinder doesn't come back out.","Pull one plug wire by the boot \u2014 never the wire itself. Twist a quarter-turn first to break the boot seal.","Loosen and remove the plug with a 5/8 in. plug socket and 6 in. extension. Inspect the old plug: tan tip is healthy, black sooty tip is rich running, white tip is lean or overheated.","Set the gap on the new plug to **0.035 in.** with a feeler or wire gauge. Confirm \u2014 every new plug ships from the factory pre-gapped to something, and \"pre-gapped\" doesn't mean \"right.\"","Apply a thin smear of anti-seize to the plug threads only (not the electrode). Hand-thread the plug fully \u2014 if it doesn't spin in by hand, stop and check the threads.","Torque to **26\u201330 ft-lb** on cast iron heads.","Reattach the plug wire. Push the boot on until you feel and hear it click on the terminal.","Repeat one cylinder at a time. Doing it one-at-a-time prevents wire-crossing.","Start the engine. It should fire cleanly. A rough idle that smooths out in 30 seconds is normal \u2014 the new plugs are burning off oil residue."],"pros":["High-value, low-cost tune-up","Catches developing problems (oil-fouled plug = ring or valve seal issue; carbon-bridged plug = idle running too cool)","30-minute job once you have the rhythm"],"cons":["Prone to cross-threading on a head that's seen decades of heat cycles \u2014 go slow and hand-thread every plug","Wire crossover (firing order wrong) is the #1 failure mode for first-time CJ owners; pull one at a time"]},{"id":"recovery-gear-cj7","title":"Recovery Gear Starter Kit \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"recovery","subcategory":"gear","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":560},"tools_required":["none \u2014 gear selection"],"skills_required":["basic recovery technique"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["recovery","snatch-strap","hi-lift","traction-boards","shackles","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB 20-foot kinetic recovery strap (20,000 lb MBS)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"3/4\" rated shackles (pair, 4.75-ton)","vendor":"Crosby / Titan","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"MAXTRAX traction boards (pair)","vendor":"MAXTRAX","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Hi-Lift Jack 48-inch","vendor":"Hi-Lift","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Hi-Lift receiver mount","vendor":"Hi-Lift","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Off-Road Recovery Gear Guide \u2014 ARB","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/arb-recovery-gear/"},{"title":"Hi-Lift Jack Safety Instructions","url":"https://www.hi-lift.com/instructions"}],"alternatives":["winch-cj7"],"summary":"At 3,200\u20133,500 lbs, the CJ-7 is light enough that a snatch strap pull from a similarly-sized rig gets it unstuck quickly \u2014 but the Hi-Lift jack is the recovery tool you'll use most often on technical terrain.","description":"The CJ-7 is a light vehicle by modern standards. A 20-foot kinetic recovery strap (20,000 lb MBS rating) is appropriate \u2014 the kinetic stretch absorbs the shock of the recovery pull and is gentler on both vehicles than a static tow strap. Rated 3/4-inch shackles (4.75-ton) at both ends are mandatory \u2014 don't use unrated hardware store shackles for recovery, ever.\n\nTraction boards (MAXTRAX or equivalent) are the correct tool for mud, sand, and loose surface stucks. The CJ-7's modest weight means traction boards are highly effective \u2014 the rig is light enough that a single board per axle is typically enough. Carry two boards. They also double as a bridging surface for a Hi-Lift jack on soft ground.\n\nThe Hi-Lift jack is essential recovery gear for a CJ-7 used on technical terrain. The CJ-7's short wheelbase means it high-centers easily \u2014 both axles can be on obstacles simultaneously with the frame resting on rock. Getting a wheel back to the ground requires lifting the frame. The Hi-Lift can reach under the frame or bumper, jack the rig high enough to place a rock or traction board under the hanging wheel, and let it down onto traction.\n\nMount the Hi-Lift on the rear receiver with a dedicated receiver mount ($45) rather than strapping it to the outside of the body. A Hi-Lift mounted to the body bounces against sheet metal and damages both the jack body and the CJ's thin panels. The receiver mount is secure and keeps the jack accessible.\n\nThe Hi-Lift is one of the most useful and most dangerous pieces of recovery gear. Key rules: always jack from the frame or a receiver mount, never from a bumper that isn't rated for Hi-Lift use; chock the wheels before lifting; keep bystanders clear of the jack's swing radius; engage the jack's handle lock when not actively pumping; lower slowly. A loaded Hi-Lift that releases unexpectedly drops the vehicle immediately \u2014 keep your hands and body clear of the fall path."},{"id":"winch-cj7","title":"Winch Selection and Mount \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":520,"high":1200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drill with bits","wire crimper","multimeter"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","basic electrical"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["recovery","winch","self-recovery","synthetic-rope","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn VR10000-S (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":720},{"name":"Warn M8274-50 (8,000 lb, classic)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Superwinch Talon 9.5i (9,500 lb)","vendor":"Superwinch","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Synthetic rope 3/8\" x 50' replacement","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Frame rail gusset kit (CJ)","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"Winch Selection Guide \u2014 Warn Industries","url":"https://www.warn.com/winch-guide"},{"title":"CJ-7 Winch Mount and Frame Gusset Tech \u2014 Rock Hard 4x4","url":"https://www.rockhard4x4.com/cj7-winch-mount"}],"alternatives":["front-bumper-cj7","recovery-gear-cj7"],"summary":"A winch on a CJ-7 transforms the rig's self-recovery capability \u2014 the longer wheelbase and better weight distribution make winch use more controlled than on the shorter CJ-5, but the frame gusset requirement remains.","description":"The CJ-7 at 3,200\u20133,500 lbs with gear requires a 9,500\u201310,000 lb winch by the 1.5x vehicle weight formula \u2014 with meaningful overhead for single-line pulls on soft terrain, steep angles, or combined mud and slope. The Warn VR10000-S ($720) is the modern choice: reliable, rated with synthetic rope from the factory, compact. The Superwinch Talon 9.5i ($520) is a proven alternative at a lower price point. The Warn M8274-50 ($850) is the classic high-line option \u2014 an older design that's been in production for decades, overbuilt for a CJ-7's weight, and favored by builders who want maximum durability.\n\nDo not undersize the winch on a CJ-7 you plan to use in serious terrain. A winch that's close to its rating on every pull overheats faster and wears faster.\n\nSame requirement as the CJ-5: the front frame rails are thin at the bumper mount and cannot sustain repeated full-load winch pulls without gussets. Install RuffStuff bolt-on gussets ($85) before mounting a winch bumper. Welded gussets are stronger; bolt-on is functional without a welder.\n\nUse synthetic rope. A snapped steel cable stores kinetic energy and releases it violently \u2014 in the close quarters of a CJ-7 cab, a recoiling cable can seriously injure the driver. Synthetic rope breaks cleanly and drops rather than recoiling. If your winch came with steel cable, replace it with 3/8-inch x 50-foot synthetic ($95 from Warn or equivalent).\n\nMount the winch handheld controller inside the cab where you can operate the winch from a seated position with the door closed. The CJ-7's cab is larger than the CJ-5's \u2014 there's a reasonable place for a bracket on the dashboard or A-pillar. Operating the winch while standing in front of the rig is a practice to minimize; control from inside the cab whenever the line routing allows."},{"id":"steering-box-cj7","title":"Steering Box Upgrade \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"steering","subcategory":"steering-box","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":240,"high":540},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","steering wheel puller","pitman arm puller","line wrench set"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["steering","steering-box","saginaw","borgeson","agr","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Borgeson 800 series steering box (CJ-7)","vendor":"Borgeson","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"AGR Rock Ram steering box (CJ-7)","vendor":"AGR Performance Steering","price_estimate_usd":495},{"name":"Rebuilt Saginaw 800 box (late CJ-7 or Wagoneer donor)","vendor":"NAPA Reman / local rebuild","price_estimate_usd":240}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Steering Box Application Guide \u2014 Borgeson","url":"https://www.borgeson.com/application/jeep-cj7/"},{"title":"AGR CJ-7 Rock Ram Application Notes","url":"https://www.agrsteering.com/cj-7"}],"alternatives":["tie-rod-drag-link-cj7"],"summary":"A worn CJ-7 steering box is a precision and safety issue \u2014 the manual Saginaw 800 has no power assist to mask its wear, and on a rig with a front locker, a sloppy box is dangerous.","description":"The CJ-7's manual steering means feedback from the road and trail surface comes through the steering wheel unfiltered. This is actually a positive on the trail \u2014 experienced drivers use this feedback to read terrain. But worn steering components turn useful feedback into noise: the wheel wanders, the rig doesn't track straight, and lock-to-lock travel changes unpredictably. A worn Saginaw 800 box on a 40-year-old CJ-7 will have noticeably more than the 2\u20133 degrees of lash that a new box has.\n\nOn a CJ-7 with a front locker installed, the steering box sees higher than normal loads \u2014 locked front axles generate significant torque through the steering linkage during tight-radius maneuvers on rough terrain. A worn box in this situation creates a real control problem.\n\nThe rebuilt Saginaw 800 is the economical option if you can source a low-mileage donor from a 1980s Wagoneer or Cherokee, or have a local rebuild shop re-kit the existing box. A rebuild runs $240 at NAPA or similar. This is appropriate for a mild-use CJ-7 or a budget build.\n\nThe Borgeson 800-series box ($480) is a new-manufacture, variable-ratio recirculating ball box designed for CJ applications. It's tighter on-center and more precise at full lock. For a CJ-7 that sees regular trail use, the Borgeson is worth the premium and removes the uncertainty of a rebuilt box of unknown condition.\n\nAGR Performance Steering's Rock Ram ($495) is a high-effort box that provides more road feel and more precision than the Borgeson. Excellent for a dedicated trail rig; the heavier steering effort is the trade-off for any street driving.\n\nThe CJ-7's 10-inch wheelbase advantage over the CJ-5 gives it marginally better natural steering stability \u2014 the longer wheelbase reduces the sensitivity to steering inputs compared to the shorter CJ-5. This means a CJ-7 with a worn box is slightly less prone to steering oscillation than a CJ-5 in the same condition, but the upgrade is still worth doing on any built CJ-7."},{"id":"tie-rod-drag-link-cj7","title":"Tie Rod and Drag Link Upgrade \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"steering","subcategory":"linkage","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":240,"high":620},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","pickle fork or ball joint separator","pipe wrench","alignment gauge or string"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["steering","tie-rod","drag-link","death-wobble","linkage","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"TNT Customs HD tie rod and drag link (CJ-7)","vendor":"TNT Customs","price_estimate_usd":410},{"name":"Synergy Mfg heavy duty drag link (CJ-7)","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":205},{"name":"Currie Enterprises DOM tube tie rod kit (CJ-7)","vendor":"Currie Enterprises","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Replacement tie rod ends (set of 4)","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Steering Linkage Upgrade \u2014 Synergy Manufacturing","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com/cj7-steering"},{"title":"Death Wobble Diagnosis \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-tech/death-wobble-diagnosis.htm"}],"alternatives":["steering-box-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7's steering geometry puts the drag link ahead of the front axle where it's exposed to rock contact \u2014 a bent drag link from a single trail hit changes toe and can cause death wobble or steering loss.","description":"The CJ-7 uses a conventional steering linkage: the drag link connects the steering box pitman arm forward to the driver's side knuckle, and the tie rod connects both front knuckles laterally. The drag link runs ahead of the front axle where it can contact rocks on technical terrain. The tie rod passes below the axle housing and is similarly exposed.\n\nThe factory tubes are adequate for stock-height use on mild terrain. Once the CJ-7 is lifted, the angles change and the tubes are more exposed to rock contact. A bent drag link creates immediate steering pull and a bent or loose tie rod causes the oscillation (death wobble) that makes high-mileage CJ-7s nervous at speed.\n\nTNT Customs builds a complete tie rod and drag link set for the CJ-7 ($410) using 1.25-inch or larger DOM tube \u2014 the same principle as the CJ-5 version but with CJ-7-specific dimensions. Synergy Manufacturing's standalone drag link ($205) is the upgrade if only that piece is bent or worn. Currie Enterprises offers a DOM kit with slightly different geometry.\n\nAll of these kits use standard tie rod end tapers compatible with the CJ-7 knuckle dimensions. Use Moog replacement tie rod ends ($90/set) if you're keeping the existing tube but the ends are worn.\n\nA toe alignment is mandatory after any steering linkage work. The CJ-7 front toe spec is 1/8-inch toe-in. Adjust the tie rod by turning the tube in the barrel adjuster (or by threading the rod end in or out on kits that use that method). Check both sides for equal lock-to-lock travel after adjustment \u2014 this confirms the drag link is installed centered.\n\nDo not drive on a freshly installed tie rod without checking toe. A CJ-7 out of alignment will scrub through tires rapidly and handle poorly on the trail."},{"id":"lift-springs-cj7","title":"Leaf Spring Lift Kit \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":980},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","breaker bar","spring compressor","pickle fork or ball joint separator"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","suspension basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["suspension","lift","leaf-springs","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Rancho RS6588B 4\" lift spring kit (CJ-7)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Skyjacker CJ7 2.5\" lift leaf spring kit","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":310},{"name":"Rough Country 4\" CJ-7 lift kit with shocks","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Poly spring eye bushings (CJ-7 set)","vendor":"Energy Suspension","price_estimate_usd":48},{"name":"Rear spring shackle flip kit (CJ-7)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Suspension Lift Options \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj7-lift-kits.htm"},{"title":"Shackle Reversal on the CJ-7 \u2014 Pirate4x4 Tech","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/cj7-shackle-reversal/"}],"alternatives":["shocks-cj7","soa-cj7","roll-cage-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7's 93.5-inch wheelbase gives you more margin for lift than the shorter CJ-5 \u2014 a 4-inch lift is meaningfully more manageable here, though the rear driveshaft angle requires attention above that height.","description":"The CJ-7's 10-inch wheelbase advantage over the CJ-5 (93.5\" vs. 83.5\") is genuinely significant for lift safety. The longer wheelbase lowers the tipping threshold less aggressively for a given lift height, which is why 4-inch lifts are common and manageable on a well-built CJ-7 while being a serious concern on a CJ-5. A 4-inch lift on a CJ-7 puts you into 33-inch tire territory with manageable stability for a driver who understands the rig.\n\nThat said: a cage or ROPS is still strongly recommended on any CJ-7 with more than 3 inches of lift running on technical off-camber terrain. The CJ-7 is more stable than a CJ-5, not immune to rollover. See `roll-cage-cj7`.\n\nThe CJ-7 rear leaf springs mount to the frame with a shackle at the rear (aft) end \u2014 a standard configuration. Reversing the shackle angle (flipping the shackle so it angles forward instead of rearward) is a well-known CJ-7 modification that yields approximately 1 inch of lift from geometry alone, without changing the spring pack. A shackle reversal kit from Rugged Ridge ($65) is the lowest-cost lift modification available for the CJ-7. It does not increase spring rate or change the ride feel appreciably. Do the shackle reversal first, then decide if you need more lift.\n\nAt 2.5 inches, Skyjacker's CJ-7 kit is well-regarded and fits within the stock driveline geometry without modifications. At 4 inches, the Rancho RS6588B is the popular choice \u2014 includes front and rear springs and shocks. At 4 inches, the rear driveshaft angle on the Dana 20 or Dana 300 becomes steep enough to cause vibration at highway speeds. A SYE (slip yoke eliminator) and rear CV driveshaft are strongly recommended at this height. Novak Conversions makes SYE kits for both the D20 and D300.\n\nReplace the spring eye bushings with Energy Suspension poly units while you have the springs out \u2014 on a CJ-7 of any age, the factory rubber bushings are collapsed and contributing to vague handling."},{"id":"shocks-cj7","title":"Shock Absorber Replacement and Upgrade \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":140,"high":520},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","open-end wrenches","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["suspension","shocks","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 5100 shock set (CJ-7 front pair)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Rancho RS5000X 4-pack (CJ-7)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":240},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series (CJ-7 front, each)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":140}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Shock Fitment Reference \u2014 Bilstein","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/en/us/fitmentguide/"}],"alternatives":["lift-springs-cj7","soa-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7 uses longer shocks than the CJ-5 due to the 10-inch wheelbase difference \u2014 the shocks do not interchange, so confirm fitment before ordering.","description":"The same measurement principle that applies to the CJ-5 applies here, but with different dimensions. The CJ-7's 93.5-inch wheelbase and longer frame put the shock mount points farther apart than the CJ-5. Eye-to-eye length in compressed and extended positions differ from CJ-5 specs. Never assume CJ-5 shock lengths transfer to a CJ-7.\n\nMeasure the stock shock eye-to-eye length at full compression and full droop before ordering replacements. If your CJ-7 has a lift installed, use the lift kit manufacturer's recommended shock lengths \u2014 most CJ-7-specific lift kits from Rancho and Rough Country include matched shocks for exactly this reason.\n\nThe Bilstein 5100 series fits most CJ-7 applications and is available in stock-height and lifted configurations. Bilstein's fitment guide asks for year and lift height, then returns the correct part number \u2014 use it. The Rancho RS5000X 4-pack covers front and rear in one order and is a cost-effective way to do a complete shock refresh.\n\nFox 2.0 Performance shocks are appropriate for a trail-focused CJ-7 build \u2014 they provide better damping control on rough terrain and better bottoming resistance under hard use. At approximately $140 per shock, a 4-shock set is $560 \u2014 roughly double the Rancho option but meaningful improvement for a rig doing real rock work.\n\nSame installation procedure as the CJ-5: bolt-in shocks front and rear, simple eye-to-eye mount. Replace the shock mounting bushings when swapping shocks on a CJ-7 of any age. Torque to 30\u201340 ft-lbs. Verify the installed extended length is correct before driving \u2014 drop the axle to full droop with the new shock installed and confirm it doesn't fully extend under the weight of the hanging axle."},{"id":"soa-cj7","title":"Spring-Over Axle (SOA) Build \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift","type":"build_recipe","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":30,"high":60},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":2200},"tools_required":["MIG welder","floor jack","jack stands","angle grinder","torque wrench","alignment tools","U-bolt flip kit tools"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","welding","suspension geometry knowledge"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["suspension","soa","spring-over","lift","rock-crawler","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"SOA U-bolt flip kit (front and rear)","vendor":"Rusty's Off-Road","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"Custom rear driveshaft (post-SOA, CV)","vendor":"Tom Woods Custom Driveshafts","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Dana 300 SYE kit","vendor":"Novak Conversions","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"Caster correction shims (front axle)","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Extended brake lines (front and rear)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Spring-Over Build Guide \u2014 Pirate4x4","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/tech/cj7-soa-build/"},{"title":"SOA Geometry Corrections \u2014 Novak Conversions","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/soa/"}],"alternatives":["lift-springs-cj7","roll-cage-cj7"],"summary":"Spring-over axle (SOA) is the cheapest way to get serious lift on a CJ-7 \u2014 flipping the spring mounts so the axle rides on top of the spring instead of below it delivers 4\u20136 inches without buying new springs. But it is not a simple modification.","description":"SOA takes the factory leaf spring arrangement \u2014 where the spring passes above the axle with U-bolts holding the axle below \u2014 and inverts it. The axle is repositioned above the spring with new U-bolt perches welded to the top of the axle tubes. This repositioning raises the body approximately 4\u20136 inches depending on the spring arch, using the stock springs.\n\nThis is the appeal: you get significant lift inexpensively, using components you already own. The CJ-7 SOA is a well-established modification with decades of documented builds on Pirate4x4 and other forums. It is also a modification that requires careful attention to geometry, safety structures, and driveline corrections.\n\nSOA is a dedicated rock crawler build. It is not appropriate for a CJ-7 used on the highway or on mild trails. The modification changes the rig's center of gravity, driveshaft angles, and steering geometry in ways that require corresponding corrections. Without those corrections, the rig will handle poorly, vibrate, and potentially be unsafe.\n\n**Caster correction:** Moving the axle above the spring changes the axle's caster angle. The front axle must be adjusted with caster correction shims or by building a new spring perch at the correct angle. Incorrect caster causes the steering to wander on pavement and creates handling instability.\n\n**Pinion angle:** The front and rear pinion angles change with the lift. The front needs to point slightly down to maintain the correct U-joint working angle relative to the driveshaft. The rear pinion angle problem is what drives the SYE and CV driveshaft requirement \u2014 the rear driveshaft angle after SOA is too steep for a standard U-joint driveshaft.\n\n**Driveshaft:** The rear driveshaft must be replaced. The SOA lift angle exceeds what a standard U-joint can tolerate. A SYE on the Dana 300 (Novak kit, $340) plus a custom CV rear driveshaft from Tom Woods ($290) is the correct solution.\n\nDo not do SOA without a full cage installed first. A 4\u20136 inch lift on a short-wheelbase CJ-7 without structural roll protection is genuinely dangerous. The cage and SOA belong together on a purpose-built rock crawler \u2014 not as a staged build where you do SOA first and cage later. See `roll-cage-cj7`."},{"id":"tire-sizing-cj7","title":"Tire Fitment and Sizing \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire-sizing","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":520,"high":1400},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["tires","fitment","lift","sizing","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich KO2 31x10.5R15","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":195},{"name":"Mickey Thompson Baja Boss M/T 33x12.5R15","vendor":"Mickey Thompson","price_estimate_usd":270},{"name":"Goodyear Wrangler MT/R 35x12.5R15","vendor":"Goodyear","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"CJ-7 Tire Fitment Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj7-tires.htm"}],"alternatives":["lift-springs-cj7","wheels-cj7","roll-cage-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7's 10-inch wheelbase advantage over the CJ-5 makes larger tire sizing meaningfully safer \u2014 but 35-inch tires on a lifted CJ-7 still require a full cage before you consider it a reasonable build.","description":"| Lift Height | Max Practical Tire | Notes |\n|------------|-------------------|-------|\n| Stock (0\") | 30x9.5 | factory spec |\n| 1\" (shackle flip) | 31x9.5 | no trimming |\n| 2\" lift | 31x10.5 | minor apron trim |\n| 4\" lift | 33x12.5 | moderate fender trimming |\n| SOA / 6\" | 35x12.5 | full cage mandatory, tube fenders |\n\nThe CJ-7's longer wheelbase makes 33-inch tires on a 4-inch lift a reasonable choice for an experienced driver \u2014 it is noticeably more stable than the same setup on a CJ-5. However, the AMC 20 rear axle caveat applies: if you're upsizing to 33s, the AMC 20 one-piece shaft conversion (see `amc-20-problem-cj7`) should be done before or concurrent with the tire upgrade. More tire torques the driveline harder.\n\nEvery tire size increase on a CJ-7 with a stock AMC 20 rear increases the risk of shaft failure under hard use. At 30 inches with open diffs, the shafts are fine. At 31 inches with moderate use, still okay. At 33 inches with a locker and aggressive driving, a stock AMC 20 two-piece shaft will eventually fail. The shaft conversion is not just a locker prerequisite \u2014 it's the right call at 33 inches even without a locker on a rig used seriously off-road.\n\nThe CJ-7 has the same thin fender aprons as the CJ-5 but with slightly different geometry due to the longer body. At 33x12.5, moderate trimming of the front fender aprons is needed for full steering lock clearance. Tube fenders from a CJ-7-specific kit are the cleaner solution \u2014 they give clearance for 33\u201335 inch tires and eliminate the patchwork look of heavily trimmed stock fenders.\n\nTire width on a CJ-7 affects stability. A 12.5-inch-wide tire on a 15x8 rim is the standard setup. Going to a 13.5-inch-wide tire on a lifted CJ-7 increases the lever arm and raises the rollover risk. Unless the CJ-7 has a full cage and is purpose-built for competition, stick to 12.5-inch-wide tires."},{"id":"wheels-cj7","title":"Wheel Selection \u2014 CJ-7","vehicle":"cj7","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":240,"high":720},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1976-1986"],"tags":["wheels","bolt-pattern","5x5.5","fitment","cj7"],"parts":[{"name":"Pro Comp Series 97 15x8\" steel wheel (5x5.5)","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":68},{"name":"Mickey Thompson Classic III 15x8\" alloy (5x5.5)","vendor":"Mickey Thompson","price_estimate_usd":155},{"name":"Black Rock Type 8 15x8\" steel wheel (5x5.5)","vendor":"Black Rock Wheels","price_estimate_usd":62}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep CJ-7 Bolt Pattern Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-cj-parts/cj7-wheel-guide.htm"}],"alternatives":["tire-sizing-cj7"],"summary":"The CJ-7 bolt pattern is 5x5.5 inches (5x139.7mm) \u2014 the same as the CJ-5 and different from every later Wrangler. Confirm this before ordering wheels from a listing that might be indexed to TJ or JK fitment.","description":"The CJ-7's 5x5.5-inch bolt pattern is shared with the CJ-5 and some older Dodge and Ford applications. It is not compatible with the TJ Wrangler (5x4.5\"), JK (5x5\"), or JL (5x5\"). The CJ-7 is sufficiently popular that aftermarket wheel manufacturers offer 5x5.5 in numerous styles, but you must confirm the pattern explicitly \u2014 wheel listing sites sometimes return cross-vehicle results that include TJ or JK-pattern wheels.\n\nThe lug nut seat and lug nut size are also period-specific. The CJ-7 uses 1/2-20 lug nuts. Confirm any wheel you purchase includes the correct lug nut (or order them separately); avoid mixing old and new lug nuts.\n\nThe 15x8-inch wheel with 3.75-inch backspacing is the target spec for CJ-7 builds running 31\u201333 inch tires. This backspacing places the tire face slightly outboard of the fender plane \u2014 appropriate for the CJ-7's narrow fender width \u2014 without excessive poke that widens the rig unnecessarily.\n\nFor 35-inch tire builds with tube fenders (SOA or 6-inch lift builds), a 15x8 with 3.5-inch backspacing may be needed to clear the larger tire through full suspension travel. Measure the actual clearance with the tire in place before committing to a backspacing.\n\nSteel wheels in 15x8 from Pro Comp or Black Rock are the correct choice for a CJ-7 used on trail. They are inexpensive, repairable trailside (a bent steel rim can be hammered back into function), and don't shatter under impact. A set of four steel wheels costs under $280 \u2014 similar in price to a single alloy wheel of comparable size. Keep a fifth steel spare.\n\nIf you want alloy for appearance on a street-driven CJ-7, a Mickey Thompson Classic III or similar period-correct 5-spoke design looks appropriate on the CJ body style and is available in 5x5.5. Keep a steel spare regardless of what you drive."}];